--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Minecraft Console Client — CDDL-1.0, not distributed here
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This project manages that client but contains none of its source or
executables. Each managed host downloads the release from the client project's
own GitHub releases, pinned to a version and verified against a recorded
SHA-256, so the binary reaches a host from upstream rather than from here.

One fixture, packages/contracts/src/boundary/mcc-config-fixture.ini, holds a
configuration document that client wrote at runtime, kept so the config parser
and the drift comparison run against the real key surface. It carries the
section and key structure and representative values — some configured, some
pinned by this project rather than the client's own defaults — and none of that
project's explanatory text.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Minecraft, and material fetched at runtime
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is not an official Minecraft product. It is not approved by or associated
with Mojang or Microsoft.

Two things the dashboard displays are fetched from third-party services at
runtime and are neither bundled nor redistributed by this repository: item
renders, which are Minecraft game assets, and player-head avatars, which derive
from the skin a player set. Both are requested by the control plane on the
operator's behalf and proxied to the operator's browser.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Published container images
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When enabled, the release workflow is configured to publish three images: the
server, the worker, and the migrate image, which is built from the server's own Dockerfile at a different target and so
carries the same bundle. The build externalises many dependencies, which are deployed under
node_modules with their own package directories intact, but folds the rest into
an esbuild bundle, and then deletes the package directories it does not keep at
runtime. Being folded into the bundle and having a package directory retained
are independent: a folded-in dependency's licence is unreadable inside the image
only when its directory is also pruned, and at least one folded-in package keeps
its directory.

Measured rather than assumed, by running the same deploy and prune the image
build runs and building the bundle with the same options: the server bundle
compiles in fourteen third-party packages — thirteen OpenTelemetry packages
under Apache-2.0 and smol-toml under BSD-3-Clause. Exactly one of the fourteen,
@opentelemetry/semantic-conventions, keeps its licence file through the prune.
The other thirteen do not — but that is not thirteen missing notices, and an
earlier version of this file drew the wrong conclusion from it. The retained
licence is the generic Apache-2.0 text: 201 lines with no package-specific
copyright line, and none of the OpenTelemetry packages ships a NOTICE file. One
unchanged copy satisfies §4(a)'s licence-copy requirement for all thirteen, and
there is no §4(d) NOTICE content to propagate. That is narrower than saying it
satisfies everything the licence asks: §4(b) concerns modified files, and the
mapping should record that these packages were bundled and transformed.
smol-toml is the real gap: BSD-3-Clause requires its own copyright line,
conditions and disclaimer to travel with a binary redistribution, and that text
is present nowhere in the image.

The prune also strips the licence files of four packages that are not in the
bundle. Those need nothing, because their code is not distributed either.

The remediation is therefore two texts copied to a stable location in each
image, not thirteen entries: one Apache-2.0 licence mapped to the thirteen
OpenTelemetry packages, and smol-toml's complete BSD-3-Clause notice. Relying on
one transitive package incidentally keeping its directory is not a control, so
both are copied deliberately. That is outstanding work, tracked separately. Until it lands the images do not carry the notices those
dependencies require, so no image should be published. The release workflow is
closed for that reason: its publish job has a literal `if: false`. Re-enabling
publication through this workflow requires a commit. An earlier version instead
gated the job on `vars.PUBLISH_IMAGES`; because that context can also resolve
organisation-level configuration variables, an inherited value could have opened
it. Re-enabling should wait until the two texts above are verified present in
the built artifacts.
